Abstract = {Identifying and contacting cell-phone-only (CPO) households in an
   efficient and cost-effective manner is critical for many studies to
   ensure the representativeness of final survey estimates. With the advent
   of address-based sampling (ABS)-that is, the sampling of addresses from
   a database with near universal coverage of residential
   households-researchers have an alternative to cell-phone frame sampling
   for identifying and surveying these households. Using a large ABS
   mixed-mode study, we explore three key questions. First, how effective
   is an ABS design in identifying CPO households? Second, is there
   significant differential response across the ``matched'' (address and
   telephone number) and ``unmatched'' (address only) portions of the
   sample, given that CPO households are nearly all found within the
   ``unmatched'' sample where first contact is limited to mail only? Third,
   can traditional sex-age-race/ethnicity post-stratification reduce
   potential bias due to differential nonresponse or are additional
   measures required? For this latter question, we examine the potential
   biasing impact of differential nonresponse where CPO homes are shown to
   be significantly different in attitudes and behaviors from the more
   prevalent homes that have both landline and cell-phone access. Data come
   from a study of 87,034 Nielsen Radio Audience Measurement adult
   respondents conducted in spring 2009. The study shows that, while CPO
   homes can be reached and interviewed using an ABS design, it is
   important to assess and correct for the potential biasing effects of
   differential nonresponse.},
